    "result_text": "I've completed a thorough investigation. Here's what I found:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification is **already fully satisfied** by the codebase, and the full test suite (including the new `AcceptanceTest`) passes green.\n\n### How the requirement is met\n\nAll rejection responses with status **400, 409, and 429** originate as exceptions thrown from the request-handling path (e.g. `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`) and are handled centrally in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`:\n\n| Status | Exception \u2192 handler |\n|--------|---------------------|\n| 400 | `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, `MissingOwnerFieldsException`, `InvalidTelephoneException`, `InvalidPostcodeException`, `FutureRegistrationDateException`, `DisposableEmailException` |\n| 409 | `DuplicateIdentityException`, `CityCapacityExceededException` |\n| 429 | `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException` |\n\nEvery one of these handlers builds its body through the shared helper `detailBuild(...)` (`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java:78`), which returns a Spring `ProblemDetail` populated with all four required RFC7807 members:\n\n- `type` \u2014 a URI (the request URL)\n- `title` \u2014 the exception's simple name\n- `status` \u2014 set via `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)`\n- `detail` \u2014 a human-readable message\n\nBecause the handlers return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`, Spring MVC automatically serializes the body with the `application/problem+json` media type, and the HTTP status codes are left unchanged.\n\n### Verification\n\n- The new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (400 path, `city` omitted) asserts the exact `application/problem+json` content type and `$.status == 400` \u2014 it **passes**. (`contentTypeCompatibleWith` genuinely requires `problem+json`, since `application/json` is not compatible with `application/problem+json`.)\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nI confirmed there are no rejection paths that bypass the advice: idempotency replays return 200 (not a rejection), and all remaining inline `ResponseEntity` constructions in the controllers are 404/2xx/`NO_CONTENT`, which are outside the 400/409/429 scope.\n\nI made no code changes because doing so would be gratuitous \u2014 the existing implementation already meets the specification and all tests are green. If you were expecting to start from an unsolved state, let me know and I can double-check for anything specific you have in mind.",
